{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_71","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/implementation_notes.md'","aggregated_output":"Early MEM-stage writeback implementation notes\n\nWhat changed:\n- mem_stage now produces the single physical regfile write port directly from EX/MEM. ALU/JAL/M results write in the MEM cycle; loads write only when dmem_ready is high. Writes are suppressed for invalid instructions, traps, and x0.\n- core wires the regfile write port to those MEM-stage early write signals. MEM/WB remains the RVFI retirement boundary.\n- wb_stage is now only the RVFI rd_wdata result mux.\n- forward_unit and ex_stage now implement a two-source operand contract: ID/EX register value or adjacent EX/MEM result. The EX/MEM forward enable excludes loads and traps.\n- Added test/test_early_writeback.py covering a two-cycle ALU dependency, a load-use dependency held by dmem stalls, trap-suppressed writes, x0 suppression, and RVFI order.\n\nDeviations:\n- I kept the existing pipeline shape and MEM/WB payload intact for RVFI and cosim compatibility.\n- I did not change dmem stall policy beyond gating early load writes with dmem_ready.\n\nConcerns:\n- Manual `make cosim TARGET=bench` in this checkout defaults the C++ harness to NRET=2 unless NRET is exported, so it fails at compile time looking for channel-1 RVFI ports on this nret=1 core. `NRET=1 make cosim TARGET=bench` is the matching local invocation and passed.\n\nLocal status:\n- Verilator lint: pass.\n- Directed cocotb early-writeback test: pass.\n- Formal: pass, 53 passed / 0 failed.\n- Cosim: pass with `NRET=1 make cosim TARGET=bench`, 2 ELFs tested.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
