{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_71","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"find cores/bench -maxdepth 2 -name implementation_notes.md -print -exec sed -n '1,200p' {} \\\\;\"","aggregated_output":"cores/bench/implementation_notes.md\nRegister the IF/ID boundary\n===========================\n\nWhat changed\n------------\n- `if_stage` now owns a real IF/ID register with `pc`, `instr`, and `valid`.\n- The IF/ID slot captures only when `io_imemReady` is high, holds on downstream stalls, clears on redirect/flush, and becomes an invalid NOP bubble when ID consumes it but imem has no replacement ready.\n- `hazard_unit` no longer takes `io_imemReady`; imem backpressure is handled locally in IF instead of feeding the hazard/ID control cone.\n- Load-use detection is qualified with both `ID/EX.valid` and `IF/ID.valid`.\n- `core` wires `io_imemReady` to IF and keeps the existing redirect priority and dmem/long-op hold semantics.\n- Comments in `core_pkg` and `id_stage` were updated to describe the registered fetch slot.\n\nDeviations\n----------\n- No new bundle fields were needed. The existing `if_id_t` payload already had the required `valid` bit.\n- `flush_if` is retained as a hazard output for the existing top-level structure, but redirect is still wired explicitly into IF and keeps priority over stalls.\n\nConcerns\n--------\n- The change intentionally adds one fetch/decode cycle after reset, taken redirects, and refills after imem stalls.\n- Local `make cosim TARGET=bench` defaults the C++ harness to `NRET=2` and fails to build against this `nret: 1` core. Rerunning with `NRET=1` matches `cores/bench/core.yaml` and passes.\n\nLocal checks\n------------\n- Verilator lint: pass\n- Formal: pass, `RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl CORE_NAME=bench bash formal/run_all.sh`\n- Cosim: pass, `NRET=1 make cosim TARGET=bench`\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
