{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_11", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'rg -n \"registered direct|direct.*fold|redirect.*fold|target-instruction|direct_target|redirect_fold\" cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'", "aggregated_output": "28:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261003-003-r9s2\", \"title\": \"Same-cycle redirect target fetch\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"Prior predictor attempts show that CoreMark benefits from removing taken\\ncontrol-flow bubbles, but every design that predicts a new PC before EX pays\\ntoo much timing in the IF next-PC, decode, or redirect-control cones. The\\nexisting core already computes the exact taken target in EX; the remaining\\nopportunity is to use that resolved target without adding a learned lookup or\\nprediction metadata to the steady-state fetch path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Prior predictor attempts show that CoreMark benefits from removing taken\\ncontrol-flow bubbles, but every design that predicts a new PC before EX pays\\ntoo much timing in the IF next-PC, decode, or redirect-control cones. The\\nexisting core already computes the exact taken target in EX; the remaining\\nopportunity is to use that resolved target without adding a learned lookup or\\nprediction metadata to the steady-state fetch path.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a zero-table redirect-target fetch fold. When EX resolves a taken branch\\nor jump and the instruction bus is ready, drive io_imemAddr directly with the\\nresolved redirect target during that same cycle, present io_imemData as the\\nIF/ID payload for the target PC, suppress the normal redirect bubble, and\\nadvance the PC register to target+4. If the instruction bus is not ready, a\\ndata-memory/long-operation hold blocks ID acceptance, or any alignment trap is\\ninvolved, fall back to the current redirect-to-target plus flushed bubble.\\n\\nThis acts as a minimal resolved-target predictor: it predicts only that the\\njust-resolved taken target can be consumed immediately, not branch direction\\nor future targets. It should recover most of the cycle reduction seen in\\nearlier loop/backedge predictors while avoiding BHT/BTB tags, counters,\\ncarried predicted-PC fields, and EX-vs-prediction compares. The Fmax risk is a\\nsmall redirect-target mux on the imem address path and carefully gated flush\\ncon\n…[28643 of 31715 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:d5a227e282c3f82e]…\n 36.672, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4204420, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 3327, \"ff\": 1050, \"seeds\": [34.778, 36.672, 36.672], \"perturbations\": [[0, 34.778, 32], [1, 36.672, 32], [2, 36.672, 32]], \"lutram\": 8, \"bsram\": 6, \"dsp\": 2, \"critical_path\": {\"from\": \"cpu/u_rf/regs_regs_0_0_s0\", \"to\": \"cpu/u_id/reg_q.direct_target_next_31_s0\", \"slack_ns\": -23.754, \"arrival_ns\": 34.052, \"logic_cells\": 61, \"modules\": [\"clock_ibuf\", \"cpu/u_id\", \"cpu/u_ex\", \"cpu/u_if\", \"cpu/u_ex\", \"cpu/u_if\", \"cpu/u_id/u_imm\", \"cpu/u_id\"]}, \"logic_levels\": 32, \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-10-03T10:02:43.800813Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 13, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-003-r13s2 (regression, -28.53%): Prechecking BTIC hits in ID still regresses when `btic_*` sideband state, `redirect_fill_accept`, and IF/ID instruction injection remain tied to EX redirect and imem_ready control, so target-cache wins must remove the redirect-cycle acceptance cone too.\"}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
