{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_8", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'tail -n 20 cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'", "aggregated_output": "{\"id\": \"hyp-20261003-003-r8s2\", \"title\": \"Redirect-fill branch target instruction cache\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"Prior branch predictors recovered CoreMark loop cycles but lost the round in timing: every version that changed the fetch PC before the branch reached EX added target lookup, immediate add, prediction metadata, or recovery compares to a sensitive feedback path. The core still pays a real bubble on repeated taken backedges and jumps, so the next predictor attempt should remove that bubble without making the live IF next-PC choice smarter.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Prior branch predictors recovered CoreMark loop cycles but lost the round in timing: every version that changed the fetch PC before the branch reached EX added target lookup, immediate add, prediction metadata, or recovery compares to a sensitive feedback path. The core still pays a real bubble on repeated taken backedges and jumps, so the next predictor attempt should remove that bubble without making the live IF next-PC choice smarter.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a tiny branch-target instruction cache that is used only on an already-resolved EX redirect. When a taken branch or jump redirects to a target, remember the control-flow PC, the resolved target PC, and the first instruction word fetched at that target. On a later redirect from the same PC to the same target, inject the cached target instruction into the ID/EX capture slot during the redirect cycle and update the fetch PC to target+4 instead of target.\\n\\nThis is a predictor because it predicts the target instruction payload for recurring taken control flow, but it does not predict direction or select a speculative next PC in the normal IF cycle. Correctness falls back to the existing redirect path on cache miss, target mismatch, stalls that cannot accept the fill, or untrained entries. The expected win is the same one-cycle loop-backedge savings seen in earlier predictors, plus occasional hiding of target-side imem stalls, with far less Fmax exposure because ther\n…[109499 of 112571 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:af7c498ef5e0b7c9]…\n\"fitness\": 133.91, \"delta_pct\": 2.87, \"vs_baseline\": 1004.87, \"fmax_mhz\": 55.62, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4153381, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 2844, \"ff\": 729, \"seeds\": [52.395, 55.62, 55.62], \"perturbations\": [[0, 52.395, 12], [1, 55.62, 14], [2, 55.62, 14]], \"lutram\": 8, \"bsram\": 6, \"dsp\": 2, \"critical_path\": {\"from\": \"cpu/u_rf/regs_rs2_regs_rs2_0_0_s0\", \"to\": \"dmem_3_dmem_3_0_0_s\", \"slack_ns\": -14.086, \"arrival_ns\": 24.381, \"logic_cells\": 12, \"modules\": [\"clock_ibuf\", \"cpu/u_id\", \"cpu/u_ex\", \"cpu/u_ex/u_alu\", \"cpu/u_ex\"]}, \"logic_levels\": 12, \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-10-03T10:28:41.675426Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 15, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-001-r15s0 (regression, +2.87%): Duplicating the async GPR array into keep-protected rs1/rs2 banks can lose because doubled reset/write routing and blocked storage sharing outweigh read-mux fanout relief; the write-first RF mux/placement cone remains the binding cost.\"}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
