{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,240p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-003-r2s2.yaml\"","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261003-003-r2s2\ntitle: \"Tiny trained branch target predictor\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The current core still resolves all taken branches and jumps in EX, so loop\n  backedges in CoreMark inject redirect bubbles even though the same PCs repeat\n  many times. The previous static predictor showed that removing those bubbles\n  can reduce CoreMark cycles, but it put immediate decode and target arithmetic\n  directly on the IF next-PC path and destroyed Fmax. A predictor that learns\n  resolved targets and reuses registered targets should keep the timing cost\n  much lower.\nhypothesis: |\n  Add a very small direct-mapped branch target buffer with 2-bit direction\n  counters, trained only from resolved EX-stage control-flow instructions.\n  IF predicts a non-linear next PC only on a tag hit with a taken counter, using\n  the stored target rather than recomputing B/J immediates in fetch. Carry the\n  predicted next PC through IF/ID and ID/EX, and have EX redirect only when the\n  resolved next PC differs from the prediction. This should recover most\n  repeated loop-backedge bubbles seen in CoreMark while avoiding the failed\n  static predictor's wide immediate-add path in IF.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: \"Add predicted-next-PC metadata to IF/ID and ID/EX payloads so EX can compare prediction against the resolved next PC.\"\n  - file: rtl/00_core_pkg_first.sv\n    description: \"Mirror the core_pkg.sv pipeline payload metadata for wildcard-ordered tool invocations.\"\n  - file: rtl/branch_predictor.sv\n    description: \"Add a tiny direct-mapped BTB/BHT that predicts from stored targets and updates on resolved branches and jumps.\"\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: \"Use the BTB/BHT prediction for the next PC when it hits, while preserving redirect and stall priority.\"\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: \"Latch predicted next-PC metadata into ID/EX alongside the fetched instruction.\"\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: \"Compute the resolved next PC for branches and jumps, report predictor update information, and redirect only on prediction mismatch.\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Wire predictor lookup/update signals between IF, EX, and the new branch predictor without changing the top-level IO contract.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_11", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'tail -n 5 cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'", "aggregated_output": "{\"id\": \"hyp-20261003-001-r1s0\", \"title\": \"Move DIV/REM off EX critical path\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The baseline timing path runs from MEM/WB forwarding control through the EX\\noperand muxes into the ALU and reaches about 150 logic levels. The current\\nALU contains fully combinational RV32M divide and remainder operators, so even\\nordinary forwarded ADD/load-address operations share timing with the largest\\narithmetic cone. Removing the divider from the single-cycle EX path should\\nraise Fmax by far more than the acceptance threshold, while CoreMark is\\nexpected to execute few or no DIV/REM instructions in the measured loop.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The baseline timing path runs from MEM/WB forwarding control through the EX\\noperand muxes into the ALU and reaches about 150 logic levels. The current\\nALU contains fully combinational RV32M divide and remainder operators, so even\\nordinary forwarded ADD/load-address operations share timing with the largest\\narithmetic cone. Removing the divider from the single-cycle EX path should\\nraise Fmax by far more than the acceptance threshold, while CoreMark is\\nexpected to execute few or no DIV/REM instructions in the measured loop.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the combinational DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU logic in the ALU with a small\\niterative divider/remainder unit that stalls the in-order pipeline only while\\none of those rare operations is in EX. Keep ADD/SUB/logic/shift/compare and\\nDSP-backed MUL/MULH operations single-cycle, and have EX/MEM capture the\\nDIV/REM result only on the unit's done pulse. This should cut the current\\nALU-forwarding critical path while preserving exact RV32M results and RVFI\\nretirement order.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 30, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove real combinational divide/remainder operators from the timed ALU path, leaving only single-cycle non-DIV arithmetic and the existing RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS substitutions.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mdiv_unit.sv\", \"desc\n…[21295 of 24367 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:51d029164afb21ae]…\ny or RVFI ports.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 108.59, \"delta_pct\": -1.31, \"vs_baseline\": 795.96, \"fmax_mhz\": 52.181, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4805217, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 2668, \"ff\": 760, \"seeds\": [48.992, 52.181, 52.181], \"perturbations\": [[0, 48.992, 13], [1, 52.181, 13], [2, 52.181, 13]], \"lutram\": 8, \"bsram\": 6, \"dsp\": 6, \"critical_path\": {\"from\": \"cpu/u_rf/regs_regs_0_0_s0\", \"to\": \"dmem_0_dmem_0_0_0_s\", \"slack_ns\": -15.412, \"arrival_ns\": 25.706, \"logic_cells\": 13, \"modules\": [\"clock_ibuf\", \"cpu/u_id\", \"cpu/u_ex\", \"cpu/u_ex/u_alu\", \"cpu/u_ex\"]}, \"logic_levels\": 13, \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-10-03T04:40:58.217236Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 2, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-002-r2s1 (regression, -1.31%): Fully registering IF/ID can lose on this single-retire CoreMark core when the extra fetch/decode slot and redirect flush latency outweigh removing imem_ready from the load-use/IDEX control cone.\"}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
