{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,220p' cores/bench/LESSONS.md\"","aggregated_output":"- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-001-r1s0 (improvement, +807.84%): Moving RV32M DIV/REM out of the combinational ALU and into an iterative EX stall path can unlock large CoreMark gains when division is cold, because forwarded ADD/load-address ops stop sharing the divider critical cone.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-002-r1s1 (regression, +711.14%): Splitting all RV32M operations into an EX-stalling sidecar can backfire on CoreMark because MUL/MULH are hot enough that holding IF/ID/IDEX around a registered result path overwhelms the ALU-mux timing relief.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-003-r1s2 (regression, -0.99%): A static backward-branch/JAL predictor can lose on CoreMark when immediate decode, target add, and alignment checks sit directly on the IF next-PC path, so loop-backedge bubble savings must beat fetch timing cost.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-001-r2s0 (improvement, +5.31%): Carrying redirect/imem kills with IF/ID.valid instead of rewriting IF instr to NOP shortens the EX redirect-to-hazard-to-ID/EX control cone while still suppressing wrong-path load-use stalls.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-002-r2s1 (regression, -1.31%): Fully registering IF/ID can lose on this single-retire CoreMark core when the extra fetch/decode slot and redirect flush latency outweigh removing imem_ready from the load-use/IDEX control cone.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-003-r2s2 (regression, -13.41%): A tiny trained BTB/BHT still puts tag compare, counter decode, and target muxing on the IF PC-to-next-PC path, so registered targets alone do not make CoreMark loop prediction cheap enough.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-001-r3s0 (regression, -0.97%): After DIV/REM leave the ALU, a decoded simple_add bypass can regress because its extra EX operand/control muxing and duplicate adder fanout sit on the same forwarded-result/load-address cone CoreMark already stresses.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-002-r3s1 (placement_failed, n/a): Store-buffer arbitration that muxes registered addr/data/mask onto the dmem port can break Gowin BRAM inference for the byte-lane dmem wrapper, exploding a 2Kx8 lane into 16,384 DFFs.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-003-r3s2 (regression, -34.57%): Even RET-only prediction can be too expensive when exact JALR return decode from imem_data and ras_top selection feed the IF next-PC path; CoreMark return bubbles did not repay the added fetch compare/mux cone.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-001-r4s0 (improvement, +5.32%): Sharing RV32M MUL/MULH/MULHU/MULHSU through one opcode-signed 33x33 product can relieve forwarded-operand fanout and ALU result-mux pressure while preserving the single-cycle multiply latency CoreMark needs.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-002-r4s1 (regression, -7.51%): A fall-through IF queue can lose on CoreMark when its count/valid and PC/instr muxing remain combinational into decode, so hiding random imem bubbles is not worth lengthening the front-end load-use/redirect surface.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-003-r4s2 (regression, -16.43%): Even a single-entry learned loop predictor can lose when the IF PC compare/target mux and carried predicted-next-PC mismatch logic cost more timing than CoreMark saves from removing taken-backedge bubbles.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-001-r5s0 (regression, +2.02%): A dedicated EX/MEM mem_addr_result adder can regress once the full ALU result is no longer the binding dmem-address cost, because extra rs1/imm fanout, pipeline bits, and address routing outweigh the false-dependency removal.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-003-r5s2 (regression, -2.88%): Decode-stage backward prediction still regresses when IF/ID is combinational, because decoder/imm_gen target add and alignment logic remain on the PC update path while CoreMark backedge bubbles save too little to repay that timing cost.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
