{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_9", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"jq -r 'select(.id==\\\"hyp-20261003-001-r3s0\\\") | {title,motivation,hypothesis,changes,outcome,fitness,fmax_mhz,critical_path,lesson}' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"", "aggregated_output": "{\n  \"title\": \"Bypass full ALU for address adds\",\n  \"motivation\": \"The current champion already removed the combinational divider and the\\nremaining Gowin path is a short but hot cone from register data through\\nforwarding, EX operand selection, and the ALU. CoreMark spends many cycles\\non loads, stores, ADDI-style pointer bumps, and AUIPC/JAL address arithmetic\\nthat only need a 32-bit add, yet those results still traverse the generic\\nALU result mux that also selects shifts, compares, logic, and DSP-backed\\nmultiply results.\\n\",\n  \"hypothesis\": {\n    \"motivation\": \"The current champion already removed the combinational divider and the\\nremaining Gowin path is a short but hot cone from register data through\\nforwarding, EX operand selection, and the ALU. CoreMark spends many cycles\\non loads, stores, ADDI-style pointer bumps, and AUIPC/JAL address arithmetic\\nthat only need a 32-bit add, yet those results still traverse the generic\\nALU result mux that also selects shifts, compares, logic, and DSP-backed\\nmultiply results.\\n\",\n    \"hypothesis\": \"Add a dedicated EX fast-add path for instructions whose result is known to\\nbe simple addition: load/store effective addresses, ADD/ADDI, AUIPC, and\\nbranch or jump target sums where applicable. Keep the existing ALU for\\nnon-add RV32I operations and single-cycle MUL/MULH, but have ex_stage select\\nthe fast adder result directly for the common add/address cases before the\\nbroad ALU operation mux. This should shave a few LUT levels from the current\\nregfile/forwarding-to-EX/MEM timing path without changing pipeline latency,\\nbranch policy, RVFI ordering, or the iterative DIV/REM beha