{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_8", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"jq -r 'select(.id==\\\"hyp-20261003-001-r3s0\\\" or .id==\\\"hyp-20261003-001-r5s0\\\") | \\\"ID \\\\(.id)\\\\nTITLE \\\\(.title)\\\\nMOTIVATION\\\\n\\\\(.motivation)\\\\nHYP\\\\n\\\\(.hypothesis.hypothesis // .hypothesis)\\\\nCHANGES\\\\n\\\\(.changes|map(.file+\\\": \\\"+.description)|join(\\\"\\\\n\\\"))\\\\nRESULT \\\\(.outcome) fit=\\\\(.fitness) fmax=\\\\(.fmax_mhz) cp=\\\\(.critical_path.modules|join(\\\" > \\\")) cells=\\\\(.critical_path.logic_cells)\\\\n\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"", "aggregated_output": "ID hyp-20261003-001-r3s0\nTITLE Bypass full ALU for address adds\nMOTIVATION\nThe current champion already removed the combinational divider and the\nremaining Gowin path is a short but hot cone from register data through\nforwarding, EX operand selection, and the ALU. CoreMark spends many cycles\non loads, stores, ADDI-style pointer bumps, and AUIPC/JAL address arithmetic\nthat only need a 32-bit add, yet those results still traverse the generic\nALU result mux that also selects shifts, compares, logic, and DSP-backed\nmultiply results.\n\nHYP\nAdd a dedicated EX fast-add path for instructions whose result is known to\nbe simple addition: load/store effective addresses, ADD/ADDI, AUIPC, and\nbranch or jump target sums where applicable. Keep the existing ALU for\nnon-add RV32I operations and single-cycle MUL/MULH, but have ex_stage select\nthe fast adder result directly for the common add/address cases before the\nbroad ALU operation mux. This should shave a few LUT levels from the current\nregfile/forwarding-to-EX/MEM timing path without changing pipeline latency,\nbranch policy, RVFI ordering, or the iterative DIV/REM behavior that already\nproved valuable.\n\nCHANGES\nrtl/ex_stage.sv: Add a dedicated fast_sum datapath from the forwarded EX operands and use it for load/store addresses, ADD/ADDI, AUIPC, and PC-relative control-flow sums while preserving existing stall and redirect behavior.\nrtl/alu.sv: Trim the generic ALU add case so non-add operations keep using the existing combinational unit, while common add/address results can bypass its broad result-selection mux.\nrtl/core_pkg.sv: If needed, add a small decoded control bit marking simple-add operations so ex_stage does not have to reconstruct the fast-path predicate from the full ALU opcode cone.\nrtl/00_core_pkg_first.sv: Mirror any core_pkg.sv control-bundle field change for wildcard-ordered builds.\nrtl/decoder.sv: Drive the optional simple-add control bit only for validated ADD, ADDI, AUIPC, load, store, JAL, and JALR encodings, leaving illegal-default behavior unchanged.\nRES\n…[1366 of 4438 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:63632194deb78a89]…\n=1 top-level contract.\n\nCHANGES\nrtl/core_pkg.sv: Add a mem_addr_result field to ex_mem_t so the memory bus address has its own narrow producer instead of reusing the generic ALU result.\nrtl/00_core_pkg_first.sv: Mirror the ex_mem_t field addition for wildcard-ordered builds that rely on the alphabetically first typedef shim.\nrtl/ex_stage.sv: Compute mem_addr_result from the forwarded rs1 operand plus the decoded immediate for load/store instructions, while leaving alu_result generation unchanged for writeback operations.\nrtl/mem_stage.sv: Use in.mem_addr_result for dmem_addr, byte-lane mask placement, load extraction, misalignment checks, and aligned RVFI mem_addr instead of using in.alu_result.\ntest/test_pipeline.py: Add or preserve directed coverage for forwarded load/store addresses, misaligned traps, stalls, and byte/halfword lane selection so the split address path matches the old behavior.\nRESULT regression fit=124.51 fmax=55.929 cp=clock_ibuf > cpu/u_id > cpu/u_ex > cpu/u_ex/u_alu > cpu/u_ex cells=13\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
