X\\noperand muxes into the ALU and reaches about 150 logic levels. The current\\nALU contains fully combinational RV32M divide and remainder operators, so even\\nordinary forwarded ADD/load-address operations share timing with the largest\\narithmetic cone. Removing the divider from the single-cycle EX path should\\nraise Fmax by far more than the acceptance threshold, while CoreMark is\\nexpected to execute few or no DIV/REM instructions in the measured\n…[129363 of 132435 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:39c88fb28755a4ed]…\nmax_mhz\": 46.238, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4261986, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 2911, \"ff\": 857, \"seeds\": [43.163, 46.238, 46.238], \"perturbations\": [[0, 43.163, 21], [1, 46.238, 24], [2, 46.238, 24]], \"lutram\": 8, \"bsram\": 6, \"dsp\": 2, \"critical_path\": {\"from\": \"cpu/u_rf/regs_regs_0_0_s\", \"to\": \"cpu/u_btic/pending_valid_q_s0\", \"slack_ns\": -18.168, \"arrival_ns\": 28.462, \"logic_cells\": 38, \"modules\": [\"clock_ibuf\", \"cpu/u_id\", \"cpu/u_ex\", \"cpu/u_btic\", \"cpu/u_if\", \"cpu/u_hazard\", \"cpu/u_id\", \"cpu/u_btic\"]}, \"logic_levels\": 21, \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-10-03T08:05:16.021426Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 8, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-003-r8s2 (regression, -11.10%): Redirect-fill BTICs can still lose CoreMark even off the normal IF predictor path, because the EX redirect_pc/target tag check plus redirect_fill_valid gating and target+4/instr injection land on the redirect-to-ID/IF control cone they were meant to avoid.\"}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"agent_message","text":"One near-miss stands out: the dmem-address split raised raw fitness by about 2%, but not enough for the acceptance threshold. I’m checking that shape and the current RTL so the new hypothesis is a stricter refinement, not a repeat."}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"jq -r 'select(.id==\\\"hyp-20261003-001-r5s0\\\") | .motivation, .hypothesis.hypothesis, (.changes[] | \\\"CHANGE \\\" + .file + \\\": \\\" + .description), .implementation_notes, (.critical_path|tostring)' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"jq -r 'select(.id==\\\"hyp-20261003-001-r5s0\\\") | .motivation, .hypothesis.hypothesis, (.changes[] | \\\"CHANGE \\\" + .file + \\\": \\\" + .description), .implementation_notes, (.critical_path|tostring)' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"","aggregated_output":"The current champion's worst path still runs from the ID/EX operand side through the EX ALU cone to the dmem side of the FPGA wrapper. Prior attempts show that predictors, fetch queues, and broad simple-add bypasses lose when they add muxing to sensitive paths, while the winning micro-opts removed unnecessary arithmetic fanout from the existing critical cone. The dmem address endpoint is currently driven by the generic EX/MEM alu_result field, so static timing can still see the multiply-capable ALU result network on a path that only load/store address generation actually needs.\n\nAdd a separate EX/MEM mem_addr_result field produced by a narrow rs1+imm adder for loads and stores, and drive mem_stage.dmem_addr and RVFI memory-address alignment from that field instead of from EX/MEM.alu_result. Keep alu_result as the ordinary writeback result for ADD/ADDI/AUIPC/JAL/MUL/etc., and avoid adding a new general-purpose simple-add result mux. This should remove the false dmem-address dependency on the full ALU case tree and shared RV32M multiplier output, while preserving load/store cycle timing, forwarding behavior, byte-lane masks, misalignment traps, and the nret=1 top-level contract.\n\nCHANGE rtl/core_pkg.sv: Add a mem_addr_result field to ex_mem_t so the memory bus address has its own narrow producer instead of reusing the generic ALU result.\nCHANGE rtl/00_core_pkg_first.sv: Mirror the ex_mem_t field addition for wildcard-ordered builds that rely on the alphabetically first typedef shim.\nCHANGE rtl/ex_stage.sv: Compute mem_addr_result from the forwarded rs1 operand plus the decoded immediate for load/store instructions, while leaving alu_result generation unchanged for writeback operations.\nCHANGE rtl/mem_stage.sv: Use in.mem_addr_result for dmem_addr, byte-lane mask placement, load extraction, misalignment checks, and aligned RVFI mem_addr instead of using in.alu_result.\nCHANGE test/test_pipeline.py: Add or preserve directed coverage for forwarded load/store addresses, misaligned traps, stalls, and byte/halfword lane selection so the split address path matches the old behavior.\n\n{\"from\":\"cpu/u_rf/regs_regs_0_0_s0\",\"to\":\"cpu/u_ex/reg_q.alu_result_0_s0\",\"slack_ns\":-12.73,\"arrival_ns\":23.024,\"logic_cells\":13,\"modules\":[\"clock_ibuf\",\"cpu/u_id\",\"cpu/u_ex\",\"cpu/u_ex/u_alu\",\"cpu/u_ex\"]}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"jq -r 'select(.id==\\\"hyp-20261003-001-r4s0\\\") | .motivation, .hypothesis.hypothesis, (.changes[] | \\\"CHANGE \\\" + .file + \\\": \\\" + .description), .implementation_notes, (.critical_path|tostring)' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"jq -r 'select(.id==\\\"hyp-20261003-001-r4s0\\\") | .motivation, .hypothesis.hypothesis, (.changes[] | \\\"CHANGE \\\" + .file + \\\": \\\" + .description), .implementation_notes, (.critical_path|tostring)' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"","aggregated_output":"The current champion's worst path is back in the ID/EX/ALU cone, and the\nrecent simple-add bypass showed that adding another result mux can erase a\nsmall timing win. The remaining ALU still builds three independent 32x32\nproducts for signed-signed, unsigned-unsigned, and signed-unsigned multiply\nvariants. Even when CoreMark is executing ordinary address adds, the\nforwarded operands must fan out into those multiplier inputs and the ALU\nresult mux still carries all multiply results. CoreMark uses MUL often\nenough that making multiply multicycle has already regressed, so the safer\nmicro-optimization is to reduce duplicate multiplier hardware while keeping\nsingle-cycle MUL latency.\n\nReplace the three parallel RV32M multiply expressions in alu.sv with one\nshared 33x33 signed product. Sign-extend each operand according to the\nselected MULH/MULHU/MULHSU signedness, use product[31:0] for MUL, and use\nproduct[63:32] for the high-half operations. This preserves exact RV32M bit\nresults and the existing RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS substitutions, but should reduce\nDSP usage, EX operand fanout, and placement pressure on the current critical\nALU cone without changing pipeline structure or CoreMark cycle count.\n\nCHANGE rtl/alu.sv: Collapse the three separate RV32M multiplier expressions into a single signed-extended 33x33 product shared by MUL, MULH, MULHU, and MULHSU while leaving DIV/REM on the existing iterative path.\nCHANGE test/test_alu.py: Keep or extend focused multiply vectors, especially signed/unsigned high-half corner cases, so the shared multiplier is checked against RV32M bit semantics.\n\n{\"from\":\"cpu/u_rf/regs_regs_0_0_s\",\"to\":\"dmem_1_dmem_1_0_0_s\",\"slack_ns\":-13.468,\"arrival_ns\":23.763,\"logic_cells\":13,\"modules\":[\"clock_ibuf\",\"cpu/u_id\",\"cpu/u_ex\",\"cpu/u_ex/u_alu\",\"cpu/u_ex\"]}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"jq -r 'select(.id==\\\"hyp-20261003-002-r5s1\\\") | .motivation, .hypothesis.hypothesis, (.changes[] | \\\"CHANGE \\\" + .file + \\\": \\\" + .description), .implementation_notes, (.critical_path|tostring)' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"jq -r 'select(.id==\\\"hyp-20261003-002-r5s1\\\") | .motivation, .hypothesis.hypothesis, (.changes[] | \\\"CHANGE \\\" + .file + \\\": \\\" + .description), .implementation_notes, (.critical_path|tostring)' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"","aggregated_output":"The current champion's reported worst path still runs through the EX ALU cone\nand terminates at the data-memory side of the design. CoreMark executes many\nloads and stores whose only EX arithmetic requirement is rs1+imm address\ngeneration, but today that address shares the same alu_result mux and operand\nfanout as shifts, compares, jumps, and the single-cycle multiplier.\n\nA prior simple-add bypass regressed because it added another competing result\nmux on the generic ALU writeback path. This proposal instead gives memory\noperations a separate address channel that is not fed back into the normal\nALU result path.\n\nAdd a dedicated address-generation result to the EX/MEM payload. EX computes\nmem_addr = rs1 + imm for LOAD and STORE instructions using a small AGU, while\nthe existing ALU continues to produce rd results for integer ops, jumps, and\nsingle-cycle multiply. MEM then uses the new ex_mem.mem_addr field for\ndmem_addr, byte-lane masks, alignment checks, and RVFI memory reporting,\ninstead of reusing ex_mem.alu_result for memory addresses.\n\nLoads still write back from read_data, stores ignore rd, and non-memory ALU\ninstructions keep the current timing and forwarding behavior. The expected\ngain is a shorter load/store address path into the FPGA memory wrapper, with\nno extra branch predictor, fetch queue, store buffer arbitration, or added\nlatency in the CoreMark steady state.\n\nCHANGE rtl/core_pkg.sv: Add a mem_addr field to ex_mem_t so memory effective addresses can travel separately from the generic ALU result.\nCHANGE rtl/00_core_pkg_first.sv: Mirror the ex_mem_t field addition for wildcard-ordered tool invocations.\nCHANGE rtl/ex_stage.sv: Compute a dedicated rs1+imm AGU result for LOAD/STORE instructions and capture it into EX/MEM without routing it through the ALU result mux.\nCHANGE rtl/mem_stage.sv: Use ex_mem.mem_addr for dmem_addr, byte masks, misalignment checks, load extraction, and RVFI aligned memory address reporting.\nCHANGE test/test_pipeline.py: Add directed load/store tests covering byte and halfword offsets plus misaligned memory traps through the new AGU path.\n\n{\"from\":\"cpu/u_id/reg_q.rs1_addr_1_s0\",\"to\":\"cpu/u_ex/reg_q.alu_result_0_s0\",\"slack_ns\":-14.182,\"arrival_ns\":24.477,\"logic_cells\":16,\"modules\":[\"clock_ibuf\",\"cpu/u_fwd\",\"cpu/u_ex\",\"cpu/u_ex/u_alu\",\"cpu/u_ex\"]}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
