{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_5", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"jq -r 'select(.category==\\\"structural\\\") | [.id,.title,.outcome,(.delta_pct//\\\"n/a\\\"),(.fitness//\\\"n/a\\\"),(.fmax_mhz//\\\"n/a\\\"),(.critical_path.modules|join(\\\" > \\\")? // \\\"\\\"),(.critical_path.logic_cells//\\\"\\\"),(.lesson//\\\"\\\")] | @tsv' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261003-002-r1s1\tSplit RV32M off the EX fast path\tregression\t711.14\t98.31\t45.077\tclock_ibuf > cpu/u_id > cpu/u_ex > cpu/u_if > cpu/u_hazard > cpu/u_id\t38\t- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-002-r1s1 (regression, +711.14%): Splitting all RV32M operations into an EX-stalling sidecar can backfire on CoreMark because MUL/MULH are hot enough that holding IF/ID/IDEX around a registered result path overwhelms the ALU-mux timing relief.\nhyp-20261003-002-r2s1\tRegister the IF/ID boundary\tregression\t-1.31\t108.59\t52.181\tclock_ibuf > cpu/u_id > cpu/u_ex > cpu/u_ex/u_alu > cpu/u_ex\t13\t- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-002-r2s1 (regression, -1.31%): Fully registering IF/ID can lose on this single-retire CoreMark core when the extra fetch/decode slot and redirect flush latency outweigh removing imem_ready from the load-use/IDEX control cone.\nhyp-20261003-002-r3s1\tAdd a one-entry store drain buffer\tplacement_failed\tn/a\tn/a\tn/a\t\t\t- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-002-r3s1 (placement_failed, n/a): Store-buffer arbitration that muxes registered addr/data/mask onto the dmem port can break Gowin BRAM inference for the byte-lane dmem wrapper, exploding a 2Kx8 lane into 16,384 DFFs.\nhyp-20261003-002-r4s1\tAdd a fall-through fetch queue\tregression\t-7.51\t107.17\t46.53\tclock_ibuf > cpu/u_id > cpu/u_ex > cpu/u_ex/u_alu > cpu/u_ex\t13\t- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-002-r4s1 (regression, -7.51%): A fall-through IF queue can lose on CoreMark when its count/valid and PC/instr muxing remain combinational into decode, so hiding random imem bubbles is not worth lengthening the front-end load-use/redirect surface.\nhyp-20261003-002-r5s1\tSplit memory AGU path\tregression\t-0.98\t120.84\t54.279\tclock_ibuf > cpu/u_fwd > cpu/u_ex > cpu/u_ex/u_alu > cpu/u_ex\t16\t\nhyp-20261003-002-r6s1\tRegister decode-time forwarding selects\tregression\t-0.85\t121.0\t54.353\tclock_ibuf > cpu/u_id > cpu/u_ex > cpu/u_ex/u_alu > cpu/u_ex\t12\t- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-002-r6s1 (regression, -0.85%): Decode-time forwarding selects can regress when EX still carries enable-guarded operand mux fallbacks and wider ID/EX control, so removing rs/rd compares from EX is not useful unless the ALU-input mux cone also shrinks.\nhyp-20261003-002-r7s1\tSplit EX operand and ALU stages\tregression\t1.67\t124.08\t63.484\tclock_ibuf > cpu/u_ex > cpu/u_ex/u_alu > cpu/u_ex\t10\t- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-002-r7s1 (regression, +1.67%): Splitting EX into EX0 operand capture and EX1 ALU can regress CoreMark when the added branch/load-use cycle plus EX1 bypass/flush control cost outweighs cutting the forwarding mux off the ALU cone.\nhyp-20261003-002-r8s1\tSplit EX with early redirects\tbroken\tn/a\tn/a\tn/a\t\t\t- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-002-r8s1 (broken, n/a): Early EX0 redirects still need an EX1-aware RAW contract: without stalling or forwarding every producer between operand capture and writeback, rvfi_reg_check catches stale rs*_rdata even before CoreMark can measure the control-flow win.\nhyp-20261003-002-r9s1\tScoreboarded early-control EX split\tregression\t-19.62\t98.1\t50.022\tclock_ibuf > cpu/u_fwd > cpu/u_ex > cpu/u_if > cpu/u_id > cpu/u_rf\t50\t- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-002-r9s1 (regression, -19.62%): Making the EX0/EX1 split formally safe by scoreboarding EX1 as unforwardable can over-stall CoreMark's dependent ALU/control chains, so early redirects alone do not repay the deeper execute pipe.\nhyp-20261003-002-r10s1\tBypassed split execute stage\tbroken\tn/a\tn/a\tn/a\t\t\t- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-002-r10s1 (broken, n/a): EX1-to-EX0 data bypass is not enough in a split execute pipe; `redirect`, `late_redirect`, and RVFI `pc_wdata` must share one resolved-next-PC contract or `pc_fwd` catches a retired PC stream mismatch.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"agent_message","text":"A useful clue: the one execute split that lifted Fmax to about 63 MHz still missed the score gate because IPC fell, so a fresh structural idea should probably preserve the five-stage cadence. The hot path keeps showing `u_id/u_fwd -> u_ex/u_alu -> u_ex`, which points at the forwarded operand and ALU result structure rather than fetch prediction."}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"jq -r 'select(.id==\\\"hyp-20261003-002-r5s1\\\") | .hypothesis.hypothesis, \\\"---\\\", (.changes[] | .file + \\\": \\\" + .description), \\\"---\\\", (.critical_path|tostring), \\\"---\\\", (.implementation_notes // \\\"\\\")' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"jq -r 'select(.id==\\\"hyp-20261003-002-r5s1\\\") | .hypothesis.hypothesis, \\\"---\\\", (.changes[] | .file + \\\": \\\" + .description), \\\"---\\\", (.critical_path|tostring), \\\"---\\\", (.implementation_notes // \\\"\\\")' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"","aggregated_output":"Add a dedicated address-generation result to the EX/MEM payload. EX computes\nmem_addr = rs1 + imm for LOAD and STORE instructions using a small AGU, while\nthe existing ALU continues to produce rd results for integer ops, jumps, and\nsingle-cycle multiply. MEM then uses the new ex_mem.mem_addr field for\ndmem_addr, byte-lane masks, alignment checks, and RVFI memory reporting,\ninstead of reusing ex_mem.alu_result for memory addresses.\n\nLoads still write back from read_data, stores ignore rd, and non-memory ALU\ninstructions keep the current timing and forwarding behavior. The expected\ngain is a shorter load/store address path into the FPGA memory wrapper, with\nno extra branch predictor, fetch queue, store buffer arbitration, or added\nlatency in the CoreMark steady state.\n\n---\nrtl/core_pkg.sv: Add a mem_addr field to ex_mem_t so memory effective addresses can travel separately from the generic ALU result.\nrtl/00_core_pkg_first.sv: Mirror the ex_mem_t field addition for wildcard-ordered tool invocations.\nrtl/ex_stage.sv: Compute a dedicated rs1+imm AGU result for LOAD/STORE instructions and capture it into EX/MEM without routing it through the ALU result mux.\nrtl/mem_stage.sv: Use ex_mem.mem_addr for dmem_addr, byte masks, misalignment checks, load extraction, and RVFI aligned memory address reporting.\ntest/test_pipeline.py: Add directed load/store tests covering byte and halfword offsets plus misaligned memory traps through the new AGU path.\n---\n{\"from\":\"cpu/u_id/reg_q.rs1_addr_1_s0\",\"to\":\"cpu/u_ex/reg_q.alu_result_0_s0\",\"slack_ns\":-14.182,\"arrival_ns\":24.477,\"logic_cells\":16,\"modules\":[\"clock_ibuf\",\"cpu/u_fwd\",\"cpu/u_ex\",\"cpu/u_ex/u_alu\",\"cpu/u_ex\"]}\n---\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"jq -r 'select(.id==\\\"hyp-20261003-002-r7s1\\\") | .hypothesis.hypothesis, \\\"---\\\", (.changes[] | .file + \\\": \\\" + .description), \\\"---\\\", (.critical_path|tostring), \\\"---\\\", (.implementation_notes // \\\"\\\")' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"jq -r 'select(.id==\\\"hyp-20261003-002-r7s1\\\") | .hypothesis.hypothesis, \\\"---\\\", (.changes[] | .file + \\\": \\\" + .description), \\\"---\\\", (.critical_path|tostring), \\\"---\\\", (.implementation_notes // \\\"\\\")' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"","aggregated_output":"Insert a narrow registered operand stage between ID and the existing ALU work.\nID/EX continues to decode and read the register file, but a new EX0/EX1\nboundary captures the fully selected rs1/rs2 operands, immediate, PC, control,\nand destination fields after forwarding. The following EX1 stage then performs\nALU, branch compare/target, jump target, and mdiv start/capture using those\nregistered operands and writes the existing EX/MEM payload.\n\nThis changes the pipeline from IF/ID/EX/MEM/WB to IF/ID/EX0/EX1/MEM/WB for\nall normal instructions. The goal is to cut the current critical path roughly\nat the EX operand mux output: forwarding compare and mux selection settle into\nEX0 registers, while the ALU and branch logic get a fresh cycle. Hazard\ncontrol must treat the new EX0 and EX1 stages as possible producers, add one\nextra bypass source for EX1/MEM results, and flush both younger stages on\nredirects. CoreMark's extra cycle on taken branches and load-use replays is\nexpected to be smaller than the Fmax gain from removing the regfile/forwarding\nhalf of the path from the ALU timing cone.\n\n---\nrtl/core_pkg.sv: Add an EX0/EX1 pipeline payload type carrying registered forwarded operands, immediate, PC, destination metadata, control, and instruction bits.\nrtl/00_core_pkg_first.sv: Mirror the new EX0/EX1 payload typedef so wildcard-ordered SystemVerilog builds keep compiling.\nrtl/ex_stage.sv: Restructure execute into an operand-capture stage followed by a registered-operand ALU/branch/mdiv stage that produces the existing EX/MEM payload.\nrtl/forward_unit.sv: Extend forwarding selection to account for the extra execute boundary and the additional producer stage without putting all compares on the ALU-input cycle.\nrtl/hazard_unit.sv: Update load-use, long-op, dmem-stall, and redirect handling so EX0/EX1 hold or flush coherently and no instruction retires twice.\nrtl/core.sv: Wire the added execute-stage state, forwarding sources, and stall/flush controls while preserving the nret=1 top-level IO contract.\ntest/test_pipeline.py: Add directed coverage for the added execute stage: ALU forwarding chains, load-use replay, taken redirect flushing, dmem stalls, and DIV/REM long stalls.\n---\n{\"from\":\"cpu/u_ex/ex1_q.ctrl.is_auipc_s0\",\"to\":\"dmem_1_dmem_1_0_0_s\",\"slack_ns\":-10.654,\"arrival_ns\":20.949,\"logic_cells\":10,\"modules\":[\"clock_ibuf\",\"cpu/u_ex\",\"cpu/u_ex/u_alu\",\"cpu/u_ex\"]}\n---\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
