{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,220p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-002-r11s1.yaml\"","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261003-002-r11s1\ntitle: \"Ready-qualified load-use bypass\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The current champion keeps CoreMark's tight dependent chains short, but it\n  still injects a full bubble for every adjacent load-use pair even when the\n  combinational dmem returns data in the same MEM cycle. Prior structural\n  attempts that deepened execute or buffered fetch improved one side of the\n  score while losing the other. A load-specific bypass attacks cycles within\n  the existing five-stage shape and avoids predictor/front-end feedback logic.\nhypothesis: |\n  Add a MEM-to-EX forwarding path for loads whose dmem access is ready in the\n  current cycle. When EX/MEM holds a valid load, the memory stage already has\n  the aligned and sign-extended load_data before MEM/WB captures it; expose\n  that value as a third forwarding source and allow the immediately following\n  consumer to enter EX instead of taking the normal load-use bubble whenever\n  io_dmemReady is high. If the load is stalled by dmem backpressure, keep the\n  existing full-pipeline hold so no consumer can observe missing data, and\n  preserve MEM/WB forwarding for all older loads.\n\n  This should recover a meaningful fraction of CoreMark's load-use stalls\n  without adding an execute stage, store-buffer dmem arbitration, or any IF\n  predictor path. The main risk is the new dmem-read/load-extract-to-ALU\n  timing arc, so the bypass should be ready-qualified and limited to real\n  load consumers rather than broadening the ordinary ALU mux cone more than\n  necessary.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: \"Expose the current-cycle sign/zero-extended load result and a valid ready-load indication alongside the existing MEM/WB register path.\"\n  - file: rtl/forward_unit.sv\n    description: \"Add a forwarding select for EX/MEM loads that can supply current-cycle load data, while preserving EX/MEM ALU and MEM/WB priorities.\"\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: \"Refine the load-use interlock so it stalls only when the matching producer is a load whose dmem beat is not ready for the new bypass.\"\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: \"Add the ready-load forwarding input to the EX operand muxes and keep DIV/REM, branch, memory-address, and RVFI operand capture semantics unchanged.\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Wire the MEM-stage load-bypass data/valid signals into forwarding and hazard control without changing the top-level nret=1 IO contract.\"\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: \"Add directed coverage for adjacent load-use ALU, branch, and store-address consumers with dmem ready, plus dmem-stalled cases that must still interlock.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
