{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-002-r12s1.yaml')\nprint(p.read_text())\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261003-002-r12s1\ntitle: \"Unified-PC split execute\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The best structural result so far was the simple EX0/EX1 split: it raised\n  median Fmax to about 63 MHz, but lost too many CoreMark cycles from added\n  branch and dependency latency. Later attempts to recover those cycles either\n  over-stalled or broke the RVFI pc_fwd contract by letting early and late\n  redirect paths disagree about the retiring instruction's pc_wdata.\n\n  That makes the most promising structural target a corrected version of the\n  split-execute idea, not another fetch queue, store buffer, or predictor path.\nhypothesis: |\n  Rebuild the split execute stage around a single resolved-next-PC token that\n  is generated once per instruction and carried through EX1 into EX/MEM/RVFI.\n  EX0 still captures forwarded operands into an EX1 register to preserve the\n  Fmax gain from cutting the regfile/forwarding-to-ALU cone. Direct branches,\n  JAL, and JALR may redirect from EX0 only when their operands are already\n  forwardable; otherwise the instruction interlocks until the needed producer\n  has a legal bypass source, then resolves through the same token path.\n\n  Add an explicit EX1 result bypass for ALU/JAL results into EX0 operand\n  capture, but do not create a separate late_redirect path. The fetch redirect,\n  EX/MEM pc_next, and RVFI pc_wdata all consume the same resolved_next_pc and\n  trap metadata for the branch or jump being retired. This should keep most of\n  the prior split's Fmax uplift while recovering enough adjacent ALU/control\n  cycles to clear the champion, with a clearer correctness contract than the\n  broken bypassed split.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: \"Add compact EX0/EX1 payload fields for captured operands, bypassable result metadata, and a single resolved-next-PC token carried to retirement.\"\n  - file: rtl/00_core_pkg_first.sv\n    description: \"Mirror the new EX0/EX1 typedefs and resolved-PC metadata so wildcard-ordered builds remain valid.\"\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: \"Retiming execute into EX0 operand capture and EX1 ALU/memory generation, using one resolved-next-PC path for redirects, traps, pc_next, and RVFI.\"\n  - file: rtl/forward_unit.sv\n    description: \"Add EX1 ALU/JAL result as a precise bypass source and request an interlock only for consumers whose producer is not yet bypassable.\"\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: \"Integrate split-execute interlocks with load-use, dmem, imem, redirect, and DIV/REM priorities without creating duplicate flush or late-redirect behavior.\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Wire EX1 bypass metadata, unified redirect control, and split-execute stalls while preserving the nret=1 top-level IO contract.\"\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: \"Add directed coverage for adjacent ALU dependencies, ALU-to-branch dependencies, JAL/JALR pc_wdata, redirects under stalls, and DIV/REM holds in the split pipe.\"\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
