{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_2", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg -n \\\"category: structural|\\\\\\\"category\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"structural\\\\\\\"|structural|split|stage|pipeline|regfile|register file|forward|hazard|if_id|ID/EX|EX/MEM|MEM/WB\\\" cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl cores/bench/LESSONS.md\"", "aggregated_output": "cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:2:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261003-001-r1s0\", \"title\": \"Move DIV/REM off EX critical path\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The baseline timing path runs from MEM/WB forwarding control through the EX\\noperand muxes into the ALU and reaches about 150 logic levels. The current\\nALU contains fully combinational RV32M divide and remainder operators, so even\\nordinary forwarded ADD/load-address operations share timing with the largest\\narithmetic cone. Removing the divider from the single-cycle EX path should\\nraise Fmax by far more than the acceptance threshold, while CoreMark is\\nexpected to execute few or no DIV/REM instructions in the measured loop.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The baseline timing path runs from MEM/WB forwarding control through the EX\\noperand muxes into the ALU and reaches about 150 logic levels. The current\\nALU contains fully combinational RV32M divide and remainder operators, so even\\nordinary forwarded ADD/load-address operations share timing with the largest\\narithmetic cone. Removing the divider from the single-cycle EX path should\\nraise Fmax by far more than the acceptance threshold, while CoreMark is\\nexpected to execute few or no DIV/REM instructions in the measured loop.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the combinational DIV/DIVU/REM/REMU logic in the ALU with a small\\niterative divider/remainder unit that stalls the in-order pipeline only while\\none of those rare operations is in EX. Keep ADD/SUB/logic/shift/compare and\\nDSP-backed MUL/MULH operations single-cycle, and have EX/MEM capture the\\nDIV/REM result only on the unit's done pulse. This should cut the current\\nALU-forwarding critical path while preserving exact RV32M results and RVFI\\nretirement order.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 30, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove real combinational divide/remainder operators from the timed ALU path, leaving only single-cycle non-DIV arithmetic and the existing RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS substitutions.\"}\n…[224106 of 227178 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:2ec417e89dfd1e83]…\ntches need source-aware forwarding/trace handling, especially under imem/dmem stalls.\ncores/bench/LESSONS.md:34:- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-002-r12s1 (regression, -25.98%): A unified `pc_token` can fix split-EX redirect/RVFI agreement, but EX1 bypassable state plus the extra branch/load-use execute slot still widens hazard/forwarding control enough that CoreMark loses more than operand-capture relief saves.\ncores/bench/LESSONS.md:36:- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-001-r13s0 (regression, -0.52%): Masking EX/MEM load forwarding with a new `valid && reg_write && !mem_read` predicate can add more delay to the hot forwarding-select/operand cone than it saves when load-use interlocks already prevent those impossible EX consumers.\ncores/bench/LESSONS.md:37:- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-002-r13s1 (regression, +0.20%): Moving load/store address generation into ID is not free when it needs `fwd_lsu_base` compares/muxing and new base-RAW interlocks; the added decode/hazard control can erase the expected dmem-address timing relief.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
