{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,220p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-002-r15s1.yaml\"","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261003-002-r15s1\ntitle: \"Early MEM-stage writeback\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The current best core is again limited by the register-file/read-to-EX ALU cone, with the reported path passing through ID, EX operand selection, the ALU, and EX/MEM capture. Prior predictor and fetch-buffer attempts mostly lost by widening front-end feedback, while split-EX attempts paid too much IPC and bypass-control cost. A narrower structural change should attack the execute operand mux directly without adding branch speculation or another execute stage.\nhypothesis: |\n  Move architectural register writes from the MEM/WB stage to an early MEM-stage write port, while keeping MEM/WB as the RVFI retirement boundary. For ALU/JAL/AUIPC/LUI results, the value in EX/MEM can drive the regfile write/bypass during the MEM cycle; for loads, the dmem read data can write only when the memory access is actually ready and not misaligned. With same-cycle write-first behavior in the regfile, instructions that are two cycles behind a producer capture the value in ID, so EX no longer needs the MEM/WB forwarding source.\n\n  The execute operand mux can then shrink from a three-source mux to a two-source mux: ID/EX register value or adjacent EX/MEM bypass. This should shorten the hot RF/ID-to-ALU path and remove MEM/WB write-data fanout from the forwarding network. The load-use interlock remains for adjacent loads, dmem stalls still hold the pipeline until a load/store is accepted, and traps or x0 writes suppress the early write, so RVFI order and architectural correctness stay unchanged even though the physical regfile updates one cycle before the instruction's RVFI retirement pulse.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: \"Expose an early regfile write port from the MEM-stage result, gated by valid, reg_write, x0, memory readiness, and misalignment/trap handling.\"\n  - file: rtl/reg_file.sv\n    description: \"Use the early MEM-stage write as the single physical write port and preserve same-cycle write-first bypass for ID reads.\"\n  - file: rtl/forward_unit.sv\n    description: \"Remove MEM/WB forwarding comparisons and selects so execute only chooses between the ID/EX operand and the adjacent EX/MEM result.\"\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: \"Narrow the operand forwarding muxes to the reduced two-source contract and keep adjacent ALU/JAL bypass behavior intact.\"\n  - file: rtl/wb_stage.sv\n    description: \"Retain the MEM/WB result mux for RVFI rd_wdata reporting, but stop using it as the physical regfile write driver.\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Wire early writeback from MEM to the regfile and route the simplified forwarding interface without changing the nret=1 top-level IO.\"\n  - file: test/test_early_writeback.py\n    description: \"Add directed coverage for two-cycle ALU dependencies, one-cycle load-use dependencies with dmem stalls, trap-suppressed writes, x0 writes, and RVFI retirement order.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
