{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,220p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-002-r3s1.yaml\"","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261003-002-r3s1\ntitle: \"Add a one-entry store drain buffer\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The current best core is no longer dominated by the old RV32M divider path;\n  its Fmax is around 52 MHz and CoreMark still runs under the benchmark's\n  realistic pseudo-random dmem backpressure. Today any store sitting in MEM\n  freezes IF, ID, and EX until io_dmemReady returns, even though later integer\n  work usually does not need the store to have physically drained. A small\n  structural decoupling point can attack measured cycles without adding a\n  predictor or another global front-end stage, both of which have recently\n  hurt this design.\nhypothesis: |\n  Insert a single-entry store buffer between MEM and the external dmem bus.\n  Ordinary stores retire into the buffer when the bus is stalled, and the\n  buffer drains on later cycles when io_dmemReady is high while the core keeps\n  executing non-memory instructions. Loads, fences, a second store when the\n  buffer is full, same-address load-after-store cases, and all UART/CoreMark\n  marker MMIO stores remain blocking so ordering, RVFI memory fields, and the\n  start/stop timing contract stay exact. This should hide a useful fraction of\n  the 22% random dStall cost for CoreMark stores with much less Fmax risk than\n  front-end prediction or a full pipeline-depth change.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 7\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/store_buffer.sv\n    description: \"Add a one-entry pending-store queue that records aligned address, write data, byte mask, and valid state, drains only when io_dmemReady is high, and exposes full/conflict/MMIO-blocking status.\"\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: \"Retire ordinary stores into the buffer on dmem stalls, keep loads and MMIO stores blocking, arbitrate dmem outputs between a pending buffered store and the current memory operation, and preserve RVFI mem_addr/mask/data for the retiring instruction.\"\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: \"Replace the current any-store dmem stall with a memory-blocked input so the front end freezes for loads, unbufferable stores, full-buffer cases, and long EX ops but can run past a buffered store.\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Wire store-buffer status, memory-blocked control, and dmem arbitration through the existing single-retire pipeline without changing the top-level IO contract.\"\n  - file: test/test_store_buffer.py\n    description: \"Add directed cocotb coverage for buffered store drain, store-load ordering stalls, MMIO marker bypass, and no double-retirement while dmemReady is low.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
