{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,220p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-002-r7s1.yaml\"","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261003-002-r7s1\ntitle: \"Split EX operand and ALU stages\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The current champion is still limited by the same short but stubborn cone from\n  regfile/read-bypass or ID/EX metadata through forwarding, EX operand muxing,\n  the shared ALU, and the EX/MEM register or dmem wrapper. Prior structural\n  attempts that add predictors, queues, AGUs, or decode-time forwarding have\n  mostly lost because they add muxing or state to that hot surface while keeping\n  one same-cycle operand-select-to-ALU boundary. A true stage split is the next\n  structural lever with enough possible Fmax headroom to clear the acceptance\n  threshold, even if it slightly increases branch and load-use latency.\nhypothesis: |\n  Insert a narrow registered operand stage between ID and the existing ALU work.\n  ID/EX continues to decode and read the register file, but a new EX0/EX1\n  boundary captures the fully selected rs1/rs2 operands, immediate, PC, control,\n  and destination fields after forwarding. The following EX1 stage then performs\n  ALU, branch compare/target, jump target, and mdiv start/capture using those\n  registered operands and writes the existing EX/MEM payload.\n\n  This changes the pipeline from IF/ID/EX/MEM/WB to IF/ID/EX0/EX1/MEM/WB for\n  all normal instructions. The goal is to cut the current critical path roughly\n  at the EX operand mux output: forwarding compare and mux selection settle into\n  EX0 registers, while the ALU and branch logic get a fresh cycle. Hazard\n  control must treat the new EX0 and EX1 stages as possible producers, add one\n  extra bypass source for EX1/MEM results, and flush both younger stages on\n  redirects. CoreMark's extra cycle on taken branches and load-use replays is\n  expected to be smaller than the Fmax gain from removing the regfile/forwarding\n  half of the path from the ALU timing cone.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 8\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: \"Add an EX0/EX1 pipeline payload type carrying registered forwarded operands, immediate, PC, destination metadata, control, and instruction bits.\"\n  - file: rtl/00_core_pkg_first.sv\n    description: \"Mirror the new EX0/EX1 payload typedef so wildcard-ordered SystemVerilog builds keep compiling.\"\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: \"Restructure execute into an operand-capture stage followed by a registered-operand ALU/branch/mdiv stage that produces the existing EX/MEM payload.\"\n  - file: rtl/forward_unit.sv\n    description: \"Extend forwarding selection to account for the extra execute boundary and the additional producer stage without putting all compares on the ALU-input cycle.\"\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: \"Update load-use, long-op, dmem-stall, and redirect handling so EX0/EX1 hold or flush coherently and no instruction retires twice.\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Wire the added execute-stage state, forwarding sources, and stall/flush controls while preserving the nret=1 top-level IO contract.\"\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: \"Add directed coverage for the added execute stage: ALU forwarding chains, load-use replay, taken redirect flushing, dmem stalls, and DIV/REM long stalls.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
