{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"jq -r 'select(.id==\\\"hyp-20261003-002-r5s1\\\") | .motivation, .hypothesis.hypothesis, .implementation_notes' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"jq -r 'select(.id==\\\"hyp-20261003-002-r5s1\\\") | .motivation, .hypothesis.hypothesis, .implementation_notes' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"","aggregated_output":"The current champion's reported worst path still runs through the EX ALU cone\nand terminates at the data-memory side of the design. CoreMark executes many\nloads and stores whose only EX arithmetic requirement is rs1+imm address\ngeneration, but today that address shares the same alu_result mux and operand\nfanout as shifts, compares, jumps, and the single-cycle multiplier.\n\nA prior simple-add bypass regressed because it added another competing result\nmux on the generic ALU writeback path. This proposal instead gives memory\noperations a separate address channel that is not fed back into the normal\nALU result path.\n\nAdd a dedicated address-generation result to the EX/MEM payload. EX computes\nmem_addr = rs1 + imm for LOAD and STORE instructions using a small AGU, while\nthe existing ALU continues to produce rd results for integer ops, jumps, and\nsingle-cycle multiply. MEM then uses the new ex_mem.mem_addr field for\ndmem_addr, byte-lane masks, alignment checks, and RVFI memory reporting,\ninstead of reusing ex_mem.alu_result for memory addresses.\n\nLoads still write back from read_data, stores ignore rd, and non-memory ALU\ninstructions keep the current timing and forwarding behavior. The expected\ngain is a shorter load/store address path into the FPGA memory wrapper, with\nno extra branch predictor, fetch queue, store buffer arbitration, or added\nlatency in the CoreMark steady state.\n\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"jq -r 'select(.id==\\\"hyp-20261003-001-r5s0\\\") | .motivation, .hypothesis.hypothesis, .implementation_notes' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"jq -r 'select(.id==\\\"hyp-20261003-001-r5s0\\\") | .motivation, .hypothesis.hypothesis, .implementation_notes' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"","aggregated_output":"The current champion's worst path still runs from the ID/EX operand side through the EX ALU cone to the dmem side of the FPGA wrapper. Prior attempts show that predictors, fetch queues, and broad simple-add bypasses lose when they add muxing to sensitive paths, while the winning micro-opts removed unnecessary arithmetic fanout from the existing critical cone. The dmem address endpoint is currently driven by the generic EX/MEM alu_result field, so static timing can still see the multiply-capable ALU result network on a path that only load/store address generation actually needs.\n\nAdd a separate EX/MEM mem_addr_result field produced by a narrow rs1+imm adder for loads and stores, and drive mem_stage.dmem_addr and RVFI memory-address alignment from that field instead of from EX/MEM.alu_result. Keep alu_result as the ordinary writeback result for ADD/ADDI/AUIPC/JAL/MUL/etc., and avoid adding a new general-purpose simple-add result mux. This should remove the false dmem-address dependency on the full ALU case tree and shared RV32M multiplier output, while preserving load/store cycle timing, forwarding behavior, byte-lane masks, misalignment traps, and the nret=1 top-level contract.\n\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
