{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"jq -r 'select(.id==\\\"hyp-20261003-002-r7s1\\\") | \\\"TITLE: \\\\(.title)\\\\nMOTIVATION:\\\\n\\\\(.motivation)\\\\nHYPOTHESIS:\\\\n\\\\(.hypothesis.hypothesis // .hypothesis)\\\\nNOTES:\\\\n\\\\(.implementation_notes // \\\"\\\")\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"jq -r 'select(.id==\\\"hyp-20261003-002-r7s1\\\") | \\\"TITLE: \\\\(.title)\\\\nMOTIVATION:\\\\n\\\\(.motivation)\\\\nHYPOTHESIS:\\\\n\\\\(.hypothesis.hypothesis // .hypothesis)\\\\nNOTES:\\\\n\\\\(.implementation_notes // \\\"\\\")\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"","aggregated_output":"TITLE: Split EX operand and ALU stages\nMOTIVATION:\nThe current champion is still limited by the same short but stubborn cone from\nregfile/read-bypass or ID/EX metadata through forwarding, EX operand muxing,\nthe shared ALU, and the EX/MEM register or dmem wrapper. Prior structural\nattempts that add predictors, queues, AGUs, or decode-time forwarding have\nmostly lost because they add muxing or state to that hot surface while keeping\none same-cycle operand-select-to-ALU boundary. A true stage split is the next\nstructural lever with enough possible Fmax headroom to clear the acceptance\nthreshold, even if it slightly increases branch and load-use latency.\n\nHYPOTHESIS:\nInsert a narrow registered operand stage between ID and the existing ALU work.\nID/EX continues to decode and read the register file, but a new EX0/EX1\nboundary captures the fully selected rs1/rs2 operands, immediate, PC, control,\nand destination fields after forwarding. The following EX1 stage then performs\nALU, branch compare/target, jump target, and mdiv start/capture using those\nregistered operands and writes the existing EX/MEM payload.\n\nThis changes the pipeline from IF/ID/EX/MEM/WB to IF/ID/EX0/EX1/MEM/WB for\nall normal instructions. The goal is to cut the current critical path roughly\nat the EX operand mux output: forwarding compare and mux selection settle into\nEX0 registers, while the ALU and branch logic get a fresh cycle. Hazard\ncontrol must treat the new EX0 and EX1 stages as possible producers, add one\nextra bypass source for EX1/MEM results, and flush both younger stages on\nredirects. CoreMark's extra cycle on taken branches and load-use replays is\nexpected to be smaller than the Fmax gain from removing the regfile/forwarding\nhalf of the path from the ALU timing cone.\n\nNOTES:\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"jq -r 'select(.id==\\\"hyp-20261003-002-r5s1\\\") | \\\"TITLE: \\\\(.title)\\\\nMOTIVATION:\\\\n\\\\(.motivation)\\\\nHYPOTHESIS:\\\\n\\\\(.hypothesis.hypothesis // .hypothesis)\\\\nNOTES:\\\\n\\\\(.implementation_notes // \\\"\\\")\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"jq -r 'select(.id==\\\"hyp-20261003-002-r5s1\\\") | \\\"TITLE: \\\\(.title)\\\\nMOTIVATION:\\\\n\\\\(.motivation)\\\\nHYPOTHESIS:\\\\n\\\\(.hypothesis.hypothesis // .hypothesis)\\\\nNOTES:\\\\n\\\\(.implementation_notes // \\\"\\\")\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"","aggregated_output":"TITLE: Split memory AGU path\nMOTIVATION:\nThe current champion's reported worst path still runs through the EX ALU cone\nand terminates at the data-memory side of the design. CoreMark executes many\nloads and stores whose only EX arithmetic requirement is rs1+imm address\ngeneration, but today that address shares the same alu_result mux and operand\nfanout as shifts, compares, jumps, and the single-cycle multiplier.\n\nA prior simple-add bypass regressed because it added another competing result\nmux on the generic ALU writeback path. This proposal instead gives memory\noperations a separate address channel that is not fed back into the normal\nALU result path.\n\nHYPOTHESIS:\nAdd a dedicated address-generation result to the EX/MEM payload. EX computes\nmem_addr = rs1 + imm for LOAD and STORE instructions using a small AGU, while\nthe existing ALU continues to produce rd results for integer ops, jumps, and\nsingle-cycle multiply. MEM then uses the new ex_mem.mem_addr field for\ndmem_addr, byte-lane masks, alignment checks, and RVFI memory reporting,\ninstead of reusing ex_mem.alu_result for memory addresses.\n\nLoads still write back from read_data, stores ignore rd, and non-memory ALU\ninstructions keep the current timing and forwarding behavior. The expected\ngain is a shorter load/store address path into the FPGA memory wrapper, with\nno extra branch predictor, fetch queue, store buffer arbitration, or added\nlatency in the CoreMark steady state.\n\nNOTES:\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"jq -r 'select(.id==\\\"hyp-20261003-002-r6s1\\\") | \\\"TITLE: \\\\(.title)\\\\nMOTIVATION:\\\\n\\\\(.motivation)\\\\nHYPOTHESIS:\\\\n\\\\(.hypothesis.hypothesis // .hypothesis)\\\\nNOTES:\\\\n\\\\(.implementation_notes // \\\"\\\")\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"jq -r 'select(.id==\\\"hyp-20261003-002-r6s1\\\") | \\\"TITLE: \\\\(.title)\\\\nMOTIVATION:\\\\n\\\\(.motivation)\\\\nHYPOTHESIS:\\\\n\\\\(.hypothesis.hypothesis // .hypothesis)\\\\nNOTES:\\\\n\\\\(.implementation_notes // \\\"\\\")\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"","aggregated_output":"TITLE: Register decode-time forwarding selects\nMOTIVATION:\nThe current best core is again limited by the ID/EX-to-ALU region, and the\nrecent structural attempts that added queues, predictors, store arbitration,\nor duplicate address adders all paid for extra muxing on sensitive paths.\nOne remaining structural cone is the same-cycle forwarding compare path:\nID/EX source register numbers are compared against the older pipeline\ndestinations, then those selects immediately drive the EX operand muxes and\nthe ALU result register. That work is predictable one cycle earlier when the\ninstruction is still in decode.\n\nHYPOTHESIS:\nMove forwarding selection from EX-time combinational compare logic into the\ndecode-to-execute pipeline boundary. While an instruction is in IF/ID, compare\nits rs1/rs2 against the current ID/EX and EX/MEM destination registers, using\nthose stages as the values that will become EX/MEM and MEM/WB when the\ndecoded instruction reaches EX. Latch the two forwarding select fields into\nid_ex_t and have EX use the registered selects directly.\n\nThis keeps the existing ALU operand muxing and all single-cycle producer to\nconsumer behavior, but removes the register-number comparator and priority\nlogic from the EX critical path. Load-use stalls, dmem holds, and DIV/REM\nlong stalls should preserve correctness because the select bits travel with\nthe held ID/EX instruction, and load-use replay recomputes the select after\nthe load advances to the MEM/WB forwarding position.\n\nNOTES:\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
