{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_1", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'rg -n \"predict|BTB|BHT|RAS|branch|redirect|loop\" cores/bench/LESSONS.md'", "aggregated_output": "3:- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-003-r1s2 (regression, -0.99%): A static backward-branch/JAL predictor can lose on CoreMark when immediate decode, target add, and alignment checks sit directly on the IF next-PC path, so loop-backedge bubble savings must beat fetch timing cost.\n4:- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-001-r2s0 (improvement, +5.31%): Carrying redirect/imem kills with IF/ID.valid instead of rewriting IF instr to NOP shortens the EX redirect-to-hazard-to-ID/EX control cone while still suppressing wrong-path load-use stalls.\n5:- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-002-r2s1 (regression, -1.31%): Fully registering IF/ID can lose on this single-retire CoreMark core when the extra fetch/decode slot and redirect flush latency outweigh removing imem_ready from the load-use/IDEX control cone.\n6:- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-003-r2s2 (regression, -13.41%): A tiny trained BTB/BHT still puts tag compare, counter decode, and target muxing on the IF PC-to-next-PC path, so registered targets alone do not make CoreMark loop prediction cheap enough.\n9:- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-003-r3s2 (regression, -34.57%): Even RET-only prediction can be too expensive when exact JALR return decode from imem_data and ras_top selection feed the IF next-PC path; CoreMark return bubbles did not repay the added fetch compare/mux cone.\n11:- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-002-r4s1 (regression, -7.51%): A fall-through IF queue can lose on CoreMark when its count/valid and PC/instr muxing remain combinational into decode, so hiding random imem bubbles is not worth lengthening the front-end load-use/redirect surface.\n12:- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-003-r4s2 (regression, -16.43%): Even a single-entry learned loop predictor can lose when the IF PC compare/target mux and carried predicted-next-PC mismatch logic cost more timing than CoreMark saves from removing taken-backedge bubbles.\n14:- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-003-r5s2 (regression, -2.88%): Decode-stage backward prediction still regresses when IF/ID is combinational, because decoder/imm_gen target add and alignment logic remain on th\n…[1416 of 4488 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:0934f9549e4f9fc5]…\nrding every producer between operand capture and writeback, rvfi_reg_check catches stale rs*_rdata even before CoreMark can measure the control-flow win.\n23:- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-003-r8s2 (regression, -11.10%): Redirect-fill BTICs can still lose CoreMark even off the normal IF predictor path, because the EX redirect_pc/target tag check plus redirect_fill_valid gating and target+4/instr injection land on the redirect-to-ID/IF control cone they were meant to avoid.\n25:- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-002-r9s1 (regression, -19.62%): Making the EX0/EX1 split formally safe by scoreboarding EX1 as unforwardable can over-stall CoreMark's dependent ALU/control chains, so early redirects alone do not repay the deeper execute pipe.\n26:- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-003-r9s2 (regression, +2.12%): Even zero-table same-cycle redirect folding regresses when redirect_fold_valid muxes the EX target onto imem_addr and IF/ID valid/flush control, because removing a taken bubble does not repay the added redirect-to-fetch/hazard timing.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
