{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,220p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-003-r10s2.yaml\"","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261003-003-r10s2\ntitle: \"Registered direct redirect folding\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  Prior predictor attempts show that the core can gain CoreMark cycles by\n  removing taken-control-flow bubbles, but IF-stage target prediction, BTB/BHT\n  lookup, RAS selection, and redirect-fill caches all added too much timing\n  pressure. The only near-miss was same-cycle resolved-target fetch folding:\n  it cut CoreMark cycles noticeably but lost most of the gain to the extra\n  redirect-target and flush gating cone. The next predictor attempt should keep\n  that resolved-only behavior while trimming the target logic it puts on the\n  redirect cycle.\nhypothesis: |\n  Add a registered direct-control target alongside the existing ID/EX payload:\n  ID computes pc+imm for conditional branches and JAL, latches it, and EX uses\n  that registered target when the direct branch or JAL is actually taken. Then\n  implement redirect-target fetch folding only for those direct taken redirects:\n  if imem is ready and the pipeline can accept the slot, fetch the registered\n  target instruction during the redirect cycle, suppress the redirect bubble,\n  and advance PC to target+4. JALR/return traffic, misaligned targets, dmem\n  holds, long-DIV holds, and imem-not-ready cases fall back to the current\n  conservative redirect path.\n\n  This is a narrow predictor because it predicts that a resolved direct target\n  can be consumed immediately, rather than predicting future branch direction or\n  doing an IF-time target lookup. It should preserve most of the cycle win from\n  the previous same-cycle fold on CoreMark loop backedges while removing the\n  EX-cycle pc+imm target add and excluding the expensive JALR/RAS cases that\n  prior predictor attempts showed are poor tradeoffs.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: \"Add a direct_target field to the IF/ID-to-ID/EX control payload so branch and JAL targets can be registered before EX.\"\n  - file: rtl/00_core_pkg_first.sv\n    description: \"Mirror the new payload field in the wildcard-order typedef shim.\"\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: \"Compute and latch pc+imm for direct branch and JAL instructions, leaving JALR target calculation on the existing EX operand path.\"\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: \"Use the registered direct target for branch/JAL redirect and pc_next generation, and expose a direct-redirect-fold-eligible indication only for aligned, taken direct control flow.\"\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: \"Add a direct redirect-fold mode that drives the registered target onto imem_addr for one cycle, emits the returned instruction as a valid IF/ID payload, and advances PC to target+4 on accepted folds.\"\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: \"Suppress the redirect bubble only when the direct fold is accepted; otherwise keep existing redirect, stall, load-use, dmem, and long-DIV priorities.\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Wire the registered direct-target fold handshake between ID, EX, IF, and hazard logic without changing the nret=1 top-level IO.\"\n  - file: test/test_direct_redirect_fold.py\n    description: \"Add directed tests for folded taken branches and JALs, JALR fallback, imem/dmem stall fallback, target+4 PC advance, and no double retirement.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
