{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,220p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-003-r11s2.yaml\"","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261003-003-r11s2\ntitle: \"ID-registered target prefetch predictor\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The strongest predictor attempts so far were the resolved redirect-folding\n  variants: they cut CoreMark cycles by fetching taken targets earlier, but\n  missed acceptance because EX redirect, imem address selection, and IF/ID\n  valid/flush gating stayed on a critical front-end feedback path. Earlier\n  IF-time BTB, BHT, RAS, and static immediate predictors were worse because\n  target lookup or immediate arithmetic sat directly on the normal PC path.\nhypothesis: |\n  Add a narrow sideband predictor that latches a statically predicted direct\n  target in ID for JAL and backward conditional branches, then uses that\n  registered target to speculatively drive imem while the branch is in EX.\n  EX no longer needs to mux its freshly resolved target onto io_imemAddr; it\n  only confirms or annuls the already-started predicted fetch. If the direct\n  prediction is confirmed, imem is ready, and there is no dmem or long-DIV\n  hold, the fetched target instruction enters IF/ID and PC advances to\n  target+4, removing the usual taken-branch bubble. On misprediction, JALR,\n  misalignment, stalls, or imem-not-ready, the core falls back to the existing\n  conservative redirect path.\n\n  This targets the near-miss from redirect folding while avoiding the expensive\n  EX-target-to-fetch-address cone and avoiding new BTB/BHT/RAS lookup on the\n  normal sequential fetch path. Keeping the prediction metadata in a small\n  sideband register rather than widening the hot ID/EX payload should reduce\n  the Fmax loss enough for the CoreMark cycle reduction to clear the acceptance\n  threshold.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Add and wire the small ID-to-EX prediction sideband plus the predicted-fetch accept/annul handshake without changing top-level IO.\"\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: \"Detect direct JAL and backward conditional branches, compute pc+imm for the predicted target, and emit sideband prediction metadata only when ID/EX captures the instruction.\"\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: \"Select a registered predicted target for one speculative fetch slot, accept it only on EX confirmation, and otherwise retain the existing redirect and sequential PC behavior.\"\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: \"Validate the sideband prediction against the resolved direct branch or JAL outcome, expose confirm/annul signals, and preserve existing JALR and misalignment fallback redirects.\"\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: \"Gate predicted-fetch acceptance with existing imem, dmem, load-use, redirect, and long-DIV stall priorities so prediction cannot create duplicate retirements or side effects.\"\n  - file: test/test_target_prefetch_predictor.py\n    description: \"Add directed tests for confirmed backward branches and JALs, conditional mispredictions, imem/dmem stall fallback, JALR fallback, and RVFI PC/order consistency.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
