{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_1", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,240p' cores/bench/LESSONS.md\"", "aggregated_output": "- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-001-r1s0 (improvement, +807.84%): Moving RV32M DIV/REM out of the combinational ALU and into an iterative EX stall path can unlock large CoreMark gains when division is cold, because forwarded ADD/load-address ops stop sharing the divider critical cone.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-002-r1s1 (regression, +711.14%): Splitting all RV32M operations into an EX-stalling sidecar can backfire on CoreMark because MUL/MULH are hot enough that holding IF/ID/IDEX around a registered result path overwhelms the ALU-mux timing relief.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-003-r1s2 (regression, -0.99%): A static backward-branch/JAL predictor can lose on CoreMark when immediate decode, target add, and alignment checks sit directly on the IF next-PC path, so loop-backedge bubble savings must beat fetch timing cost.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-001-r2s0 (improvement, +5.31%): Carrying redirect/imem kills with IF/ID.valid instead of rewriting IF instr to NOP shortens the EX redirect-to-hazard-to-ID/EX control cone while still suppressing wrong-path load-use stalls.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-002-r2s1 (regression, -1.31%): Fully registering IF/ID can lose on this single-retire CoreMark core when the extra fetch/decode slot and redirect flush latency outweigh removing imem_ready from the load-use/IDEX control cone.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-003-r2s2 (regression, -13.41%): A tiny trained BTB/BHT still puts tag compare, counter decode, and target muxing on the IF PC-to-next-PC path, so registered targets alone do not make CoreMark loop prediction cheap enough.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-001-r3s0 (regression, -0.97%): After DIV/REM leave the ALU, a decoded simple_add bypass can regress because its extra EX operand/control muxing and duplicate adder fanout sit on the same forwarded-result/load-address cone CoreMark already stresses.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-002-r3s1 (placement_failed, n/a): Store-buffer arbitration that muxes registered addr/data/mask onto the dmem port can break Gowin BRAM inference for the byte-lane dmem wrapper,\n…[5808 of 8880 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:db66a2f13f889222]…\ning redirect_fold_accept/imem-ready/flush gating still sits on the redirect-to-fetch control path, so target-add removal was not the binding predictor cost.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-001-r11s0 (regression, -0.08%): Pulling single-cycle MUL* selection out of the ALU did not improve CoreMark because the EX/MEM writeback path still needs an `is_mul_op ? mul_result : alu_result` mux and the multiplier continues to fan out from forwarded operands.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-002-r11s1 (regression, -1.58%): Ready-qualified MEM->EX load bypass can lose even with combinational dmem because the load-data source and ready/pending forwarding select widen the hot operand/branch cone more than CoreMark's adjacent load-use savings repay.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-003-r11s2 (regression, -1.07%): ID-sideband target prefetch still leaves `pred_confirm`/`pred_accept` tied to EX redirect, imem_ready, and IF/ID flush gating, so moving the direct target off the EX imem-address mux did not remove CoreMark's front-end feedback cost.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
