{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_8", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"jq -r 'select(.id==\\\"hyp-20261003-003-r10s2\\\") | {id,title,motivation,hypothesis:.hypothesis.hypothesis,expected_impact,changes,fitness,delta_pct,fmax_mhz,cycles,critical_path,implementation_notes,lesson} | to_entries[] | \\\"### \\\\(.key)\\\\n\\\\(.value|if type==\\\"string\\\" then . else tojson end)\\\\n\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"", "aggregated_output": "### id\nhyp-20261003-003-r10s2\n\n### title\nRegistered direct redirect folding\n\n### motivation\nPrior predictor attempts show that the core can gain CoreMark cycles by\nremoving taken-control-flow bubbles, but IF-stage target prediction, BTB/BHT\nlookup, RAS selection, and redirect-fill caches all added too much timing\npressure. The only near-miss was same-cycle resolved-target fetch folding:\nit cut CoreMark cycles noticeably but lost most of the gain to the extra\nredirect-target and flush gating cone. The next predictor attempt should keep\nthat resolved-only behavior while trimming the target logic it puts on the\nredirect cycle.\n\n\n### hypothesis\nAdd a registered direct-control target alongside the existing ID/EX payload:\nID computes pc+imm for conditional branches and JAL, latches it, and EX uses\nthat registered target when the direct branch or JAL is actually taken. Then\nimplement redirect-target fetch folding only for those direct taken redirects:\nif imem is ready and the pipeline can accept the slot, fetch the registered\ntarget instruction during the redirect cycle, suppress the redirect bubble,\nand advance PC to target+4. JALR/return traffic, misaligned targets, dmem\nholds, long-DIV holds, and imem-not-ready cases fall back to the current\nconservative redirect path.\n\nThis is a narrow predictor because it predicts that a resolved direct target\ncan be consumed immediately, rather than predicting future branch direction or\ndoing an IF-time target lookup. It should preserve most of the cycle win from\nthe previous same-cycle fold on CoreMark loop backedges while removing the\nEX-cycle pc+imm target add and excluding the expensive JALR/RAS cases that\nprior predictor attempts showed are poor tradeoffs.\n\n\n### expected_impact\n{\"fitness_delta_pct\":6,\"confidence\":\"medium\"}\n\n### changes\n[{\"file\":\"rtl/core_pkg.sv\",\"description\":\"Add a direct_target field to the IF/ID-to-ID/EX control payload so branch and JAL targets can be registered before EX.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/00_core_pkg_first.sv\",\"description\":\"Mirror the new payload field in the wildcard-order typedef shim.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/id_stage.sv\",\"description\":\"Compute and latch pc+imm for direct branch and JAL instructions, leaving JALR target calculation on the existing EX operand path.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/ex_stage.sv\",\"description\":\"Use the registered direct target for branch/JAL redirect and pc_next generation, and expose a direct-redirect-fold-eligible indication only for aligned, taken direct control flow.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/if_stage.sv\",\"description\":\"Add a direct redirect-fold mode that drives the registered target onto imem_addr for one cycle, emits the returned instruction as a valid IF/ID payload, and advances PC to target+4 on accepted folds.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\",\"description\":\"Suppress the redirect bubble only when the direct fold is accepted; otherwise keep existing redirect, stall, load-use, dmem, and long-DIV priorities.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/core.sv\",\"description\":\"Wire the registered direct-target fold handshake between ID, EX, IF, and hazard logic without changing the nret=1 top-level IO.\"},{\"file\":\"test/test_direct_redirect_fold.py\",\"description\":\"Add directed tests for folded taken branches and JALs, JALR fallback, imem/dmem stall fallback, target+4 PC advance, and no double retirement.\"}]\n\n### fitness\n126.77\n\n### delta_pct\n3.88\n\n### fmax_mhz\n52.591\n\n### cycles\n4148413\n\n### critical_path\n{\"from\":\"cpu/u_rf/regs_regs_0_0_s0\",\"to\":\"cpu/u_id/reg_q.direct_target_30_s0\",\"slack_ns\":-15.201,\"arrival_ns\":25.495,\"logic_cells\":60,\"modules\":[\"clock_ibuf\",\"cpu/u_id\",\"cpu/u_ex\",\"cpu/u_if\",\"cpu/u_id\"]}\n\n### implementation_notes\n\n\n### lesson\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-003-r10s2 (regression, +3.88%): Registering direct BR/JAL targets did not make redirect folding profitable; the remaining redirect_fold_accept/imem-ready/flush gating still sits on the redirect-to-fetch control path, so target-add removal was not the binding predictor cost.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"jq -r 'select(.id==\\\"hyp-20261003-003-r9s2\\\") | {id,title,motivation,hypothesis:.hypothesis.hypothesis,expected_impact,changes,fitness,delta_pct,fmax_mhz,cycles,critical_path,implementation_notes,lesson} | to_entries[] | \\\"### \\\\(.key)\\\\n\\\\(.value|if type==\\\"string\\\" then . else tojson end)\\\\n\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"","aggregated_output":"### id\nhyp-20261003-003-r9s2\n\n### title\nSame-cycle redirect target fetch\n\n### motivation\nPrior predictor attempts show that CoreMark benefits from removing taken\ncontrol-flow bubbles, but every design that predicts a new PC before EX pays\ntoo much timing in the IF next-PC, decode, or redirect-control cones. The\nexisting core already computes the exact taken target in EX; the remaining\nopportunity is to use that resolved target without adding a learned lookup or\nprediction metadata to the steady-state fetch path.\n\n\n### hypothesis\nAdd a zero-table redirect-target fetch fold. When EX resolves a taken branch\nor jump and the instruction bus is ready, drive io_imemAddr directly with the\nresolved redirect target during that same cycle, present io_imemData as the\nIF/ID payload for the target PC, suppress the normal redirect bubble, and\nadvance the PC register to target+4. If the instruction bus is not ready, a\ndata-memory/long-operation hold blocks ID acceptance, or any alignment trap is\ninvolved, fall back to the current redirect-to-target plus flushed bubble.\n\nThis acts as a minimal resolved-target predictor: it predicts only that the\njust-resolved taken target can be consumed immediately, not branch direction\nor future targets. It should recover most of the cycle reduction seen in\nearlier loop/backedge predictors while avoiding BHT/BTB tags, counters,\ncarried predicted-PC fields, and EX-vs-prediction compares. The Fmax risk is a\nsmall redirect-target mux on the imem address path and carefully gated flush\ncontrol, so the implementation must keep the normal linear PC path unchanged.\n\n\n### expected_impact\n{\"fitness_delta_pct\":6,\"confidence\":\"medium\"}\n\n### changes\n[{\"file\":\"rtl/if_stage.sv\",\"description\":\"Add a redirect-fetch-fold mode that drives the resolved target onto imem_addr, emits the target instruction as a valid IF/ID payload when imem_ready is high, and advances PC to target+4 only for accepted folds.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\",\"description\":\"Add a redirect_fold_valid input so redirect flush is suppressed only when ID can safely capture the same-cycle target instruction; otherwise preserve the existing bubble and stall priority.\"},{\"file\":\"rtl/core.sv\",\"description\":\"Wire the redirect fold handshake between EX, IF, and hazard logic without changing the nret=1 top-level IO contract.\"},{\"file\":\"test/test_redirect_fetch_fold.py\",\"description\":\"Add directed cocotb coverage for folded taken branches and JALs, imem-not-ready fallback, dmem-stall fallback, target+4 PC advance, and no double retirement.\"}]\n\n### fitness\n124.63\n\n### delta_pct\n2.12\n\n### fmax_mhz\n51.917\n\n### cycles\n4165827\n\n### critical_path\n{\"from\":\"cpu/u_rf/regs_regs_0_0_s0\",\"to\":\"dmem_2_dmem_2_0_0_s\",\"slack_ns\":-13.882,\"arrival_ns\":24.176,\"logic_cells\":14,\"modules\":[\"clock_ibuf\",\"cpu/u_id\",\"cpu/u_ex\",\"cpu/u_ex/u_alu\",\"cpu/u_ex\"]}\n\n### implementation_notes\n\n\n### lesson\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-003-r9s2 (regression, +2.12%): Even zero-table same-cycle redirect folding regresses when redirect_fold_valid muxes the EX target onto imem_addr and IF/ID valid/flush control, because removing a taken bubble does not repay the added redirect-to-fetch/hazard timing.\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
