{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,220p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-003-r14s2.yaml\"","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261003-003-r14s2\ntitle: \"Flush-free direct fold sideband\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The best predictor attempts are the resolved-target redirect-fold designs:\n  they reduced CoreMark cycles from roughly 4.49M to about 4.15M, and the\n  registered direct-target fold reached 126.77 fitness, just below the\n  replacement threshold. Its timing miss was not the target add itself; Gowin\n  reported a long redirect/imem-ready/flush cone feeding the wide ID-stage\n  direct_target register. That suggests the remaining opportunity is to keep\n  the same conservative direct-control fold while removing fold acceptance from\n  wide payload update logic.\nhypothesis: |\n  Rework the registered direct redirect fold as a small predictor sideband\n  rather than an added field in the flushed ID/EX payload. ID still computes the\n  direct pc+imm target for conditional branches and JAL, but the 32-bit target\n  is captured in a sideband register whose data input is never zeroed or muxed\n  by redirect_fold_accept, imem_ready, or flush; only a one-bit sideband valid\n  flag is killed on bubbles and redirects. EX uses that sideband target when a\n  direct branch or JAL resolves taken and aligned, and IF performs the same\n  target-instruction fold as the prior near-miss when imem is ready and no\n  dmem or long-operation hold prevents acceptance.\n\n  This preserves the measured CoreMark cycle win from direct redirect folding\n  while shortening the path that previously ran through EX, IF fold acceptance,\n  and back into ID's wide direct_target flop. JALR, misaligned targets,\n  imem-not-ready cycles, dmem stalls, and DIV/REM holds all remain on the\n  existing conservative redirect path, so correctness and RVFI PC sequencing\n  stay simple.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 5\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: \"Compute direct branch/JAL pc+imm and drive a valid-gated sideband target path without adding fold-accept-controlled muxing to the wide ID/EX payload.\"\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: \"Consume the aligned sideband target for direct taken branch/JAL redirects and expose a fold-eligible indication that excludes JALR and traps.\"\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: \"Keep the direct redirect-fold fetch path from the near-miss design, but make target instruction acceptance depend only on the resolved fold request, imem_ready, and existing hold state.\"\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: \"Suppress the redirect bubble only for an accepted direct fold while ensuring the fold-accept signal does not gate the sideband target data register.\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Wire the direct-fold sideband target, sideband valid, and fold acceptance signals between ID, EX, IF, and hazard logic without changing top-level IO.\"\n  - file: test/test_direct_fold_sideband.py\n    description: \"Add directed coverage for folded branches and JALs, loop exits, imem/dmem fallback, stale sideband target invalidation, target+4 PC advance, and RVFI order/PC consistency.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
