{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,220p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-003-r15s2.yaml\"","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261003-003-r15s2\ntitle: \"Verified return fold sideband\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The current champion made predictor-category progress only after avoiding the normal IF next-PC predictor path: direct branch and JAL targets are computed as a narrow ID sideband, then verified in EX and folded into fetch only on a resolved taken redirect.  That preserves the CoreMark cycle win while keeping the critical path near the existing register-file-to-ALU cone.\n\n  The remaining direct-control gap is JALR, especially function returns.  CoreMark still pays the ordinary redirect bubble for returns because their target depends on rs1, but a small return-address sideband can supply the likely target without putting instruction decode, a RAS mux, or a target adder on the speculative IF PC path.\nhypothesis: |\n  Add a tiny verified return-fold predictor for JALR return patterns, built in the same spirit as the successful direct-fold sideband.  Maintain a 2-entry return address stack updated by resolved direct calls and returns, capture the predicted return target as a separate ID-to-EX sideband for decoded JALR return instructions, and allow IF to fold the target instruction only when EX verifies that the predicted target equals the resolved JALR target and the usual imem/dmem hold conditions permit acceptance.\n\n  On a correct return prediction this removes the same redirect bubble that direct branch folding now removes, but the fetched PC data comes from a registered sideband rather than from the rs1+imm adder.  Mispredicted or non-return JALRs fall back to the existing conservative redirect path, so RVFI PC sequencing, traps, stalls, and the nret=1 top-level contract remain unchanged.  The expected gain is a modest IPC improvement from hot call/return traffic with limited Fmax risk because no predictor lookup is placed in the always-on fall-through fetch path.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 5\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/return_fold_predictor.sv\n    description: \"Add a tiny return-address sideband predictor with push/pop state and a registered predicted target for verified JALR return folding.\"\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: \"Detect canonical return-pattern JALRs, capture the predictor's current return target into a sideband register, and keep that sideband independent of wide ID/EX payload flush muxing.\"\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: \"For JALR returns, compare the sideband predicted target with the resolved rs1+imm target and request a fold only on an aligned verified match; otherwise use the existing redirect behavior.\"\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: \"Reuse the direct-fold fetch hook for verified return targets so IF can fetch the return target instruction and advance to target+4 when the fold is accepted.\"\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: \"Treat verified return-fold acceptance like direct-fold acceptance for redirect bubble suppression while preserving existing imem, dmem, and long-operation priorities.\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Wire the return-fold predictor sideband, resolved call/return update events, and fold acceptance signals through the single-retire pipeline without changing top-level IO.\"\n  - file: test/test_return_fold.py\n    description: \"Add directed coverage for matched return folds, RAS miss fallback, nested calls, imem/dmem stall fallback, misaligned JALR traps, and RVFI PC/order consistency.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
