{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_21","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,220p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-003-r6s2.yaml\"","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261003-003-r6s2\ntitle: \"Decode-stage learned target predictor\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  Earlier predictor attempts showed useful CoreMark control-flow bubbles to remove,\n  but the losses came from putting target computation, BTB tag checks, RAS repair,\n  or predicted-next-PC muxing directly in the fetch critical path. The remaining\n  promising predictor angle is to learn targets from resolved control flow, then\n  use those registered targets one stage later in decode where the instruction\n  opcode is already available and no immediate adder is needed on the PC path.\nhypothesis: |\n  Add a tiny direct-mapped decode-stage BTB/BHT trained by EX. IF continues to\n  fetch pc+4 by default; when the instruction reaches ID, a tag hit on a branch,\n  JAL, or JALR with a taken counter redirects the next fetch to the stored target\n  without invalidating the current instruction. Carry only a predicted-taken bit\n  and predicted target through ID/EX so EX can suppress redirects on correct\n  predictions and repair loop exits, aliases, stale targets, or changed symbolic\n  instructions by redirecting to the resolved next PC.\n\n  This should recover much of the IPC gain seen from previous BTB/static loop\n  predictors, but avoids the two costly pieces that repeatedly regressed Fmax:\n  no IF-stage immediate decode/target add, and no fetch-time return-stack or CAM\n  path. The table can be just four direct-mapped entries with two-bit counters,\n  which should be enough for CoreMark hot backedges and direct jumps while keeping\n  timing cost below the saved taken-control bubbles.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: \"Add compact predicted-taken and predicted-target fields to IF/ID or ID/EX payloads so EX can verify decode-stage predictions precisely.\"\n  - file: rtl/00_core_pkg_first.sv\n    description: \"Mirror the prediction metadata additions for wildcard-ordered SystemVerilog builds.\"\n  - file: rtl/decode_target_predictor.sv\n    description: \"Add a four-entry direct-mapped BTB/BHT trained from EX resolved branches and jumps, with decode-PC lookup and opcode-gated taken prediction.\"\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: \"Request an early PC redirect on a predictor hit only when decode sees a valid branch, JAL, or JALR and the pipeline is not stalled or flushed; latch prediction metadata into ID/EX.\"\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: \"Compare the resolved next PC against the carried decode prediction, suppress redirects on correct predictions, and repair misses, loop exits, aliases, stale targets, and misaligned-target traps.\"\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: \"Add a decode-prediction PC update path below EX redirects and above normal pc+4 advancement, without killing the current IF/ID instruction.\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Wire predictor lookup, EX training, decode redirect, and EX recovery signals while preserving the existing nret=1 top-level IO contract.\"\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: \"Ensure decode predictions are blocked under load-use, imem, dmem, or long-DIV stalls so the PC never advances under a held decode instruction.\"\n  - file: test/test_decode_target_predictor.py\n    description: \"Add directed coverage for predicted taken backedges, direct jumps, loop-exit repair, stale-target repair, stall gating, and EX redirect priority.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
