{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,220p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261003-003-r8s2.yaml\"","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261003-003-r8s2\ntitle: \"Redirect-fill branch target instruction cache\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  Prior branch predictors recovered CoreMark loop cycles but lost the round in timing: every version that changed the fetch PC before the branch reached EX added target lookup, immediate add, prediction metadata, or recovery compares to a sensitive feedback path. The core still pays a real bubble on repeated taken backedges and jumps, so the next predictor attempt should remove that bubble without making the live IF next-PC choice smarter.\nhypothesis: |\n  Add a tiny branch-target instruction cache that is used only on an already-resolved EX redirect. When a taken branch or jump redirects to a target, remember the control-flow PC, the resolved target PC, and the first instruction word fetched at that target. On a later redirect from the same PC to the same target, inject the cached target instruction into the ID/EX capture slot during the redirect cycle and update the fetch PC to target+4 instead of target.\n\n  This is a predictor because it predicts the target instruction payload for recurring taken control flow, but it does not predict direction or select a speculative next PC in the normal IF cycle. Correctness falls back to the existing redirect path on cache miss, target mismatch, stalls that cannot accept the fill, or untrained entries. The expected win is the same one-cycle loop-backedge savings seen in earlier predictors, plus occasional hiding of target-side imem stalls, with far less Fmax exposure because there is no IF-stage immediate adder, BTB counter lookup, carried predicted-next-PC field, or EX prediction-mismatch comparator.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/branch_fill_predictor.sv\n    description: \"Add a one- or two-entry redirect-fill BTIC that records resolved control-flow PC, target PC, and fetched target instruction, and reports a fill hit only when the current EX redirect matches both PC and target.\"\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: \"On a fill hit, present the cached target instruction as the IF/ID payload during the redirect cycle and set the PC to target+4; otherwise preserve the existing redirect, stall, and linear fetch behavior.\"\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: \"Treat a valid redirect fill as satisfying the otherwise-empty redirect slot, suppress the redirect flush only when ID can accept the cached instruction, and keep load-use, dmem, and long-DIV stalls as fallback miss cases.\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Wire EX redirect PC/target, current fetch PC/data/ready training signals, fill-hit control, and cached instruction payload without changing the nret=1 top-level IO contract.\"\n  - file: test/test_branch_fill_predictor.py\n    description: \"Add directed coverage for BTIC training, repeated taken-loop fills, target mismatch fallback, stall-blocked fills, target+4 PC advance, and no double-retirement under imem or dmem stalls.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
