eMark already stresses.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-002-r3s1 (placement_failed, n/a): Store-buffer arbitration that muxes registered addr/data/mask onto the dmem port can break Gowin BRAM inference for the byte-lane dmem wrapper,\n…[3285 of 6357 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:6038a21c41d9fb2a]…\nresolved_next_pc compare still add enough front-end/control timing cost that CoreMark backedge savings do not repay it.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-001-r8s0 (regression, -9.57%): Opcode-qualified operand isolation around the EX multiplier, branch comparator, and target adders can cost more than it saves because the new valid/control muxing lands on the same forwarded-operand and redirect cones CoreMark already stresses.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-002-r8s1 (broken, n/a): Early EX0 redirects still need an EX1-aware RAW contract: without stalling or forwarding every producer between operand capture and writeback, rvfi_reg_check catches stale rs*_rdata even before CoreMark can measure the control-flow win.\n- 2026-10-03 hyp-20261003-003-r8s2 (regression, -11.10%): Redirect-fill BTICs can still lose CoreMark even off the normal IF predictor path, because the EX redirect_pc/target tag check plus redirect_fill_valid gating and target+4/instr injection land on the redirect-to-ID/IF control cone they were meant to avoid.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
